// Practice quiz: Functions as values: arrow functions and callbacks
// First draft: unreviewed. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: html-css-js/javascript-fundamentals/functions-as-values-arrow-functions-and-callbacks

::o1 pass or call::button.addEventListener("click", save()); The save function runs once at page load and never on clicks. What is wrong? {
=save() calls it now and passes its return value; pass save instead #Correct. Parentheses call the function immediately, so the listener receives the result.
~Listeners need a string name #Not quite. addEventListener expects a function value as its listener.
~save must be declared with const #Not quite. The declaration form does not cause this behavior.
~click is the wrong event for buttons #Not quite. The event name is fine; the listener argument is the problem.
}

::o1 function in a variable::const f \= Math.max; What does f(2, 9) return? {
=9 #Correct. f refers to the same function value, so calling it works the same way.
~undefined #Not quite. Calling through another name still returns the result.
~A TypeError is thrown #Not quite. Functions can be stored in any variable.
~The text of the function #Not quite. Parentheses call it; they do not print it.
}

::o2 concise body::A function written with an arrow has a body of braces containing only the expression a + b, and no return keyword. What does calling it return? {
=undefined, because a block body needs an explicit return #Correct. Only a concise body returns automatically.
~The sum of a and b #Not quite. That would need return, or no braces.
~An object with a and b #Not quite. Braces here are a block, not an object literal.
~A SyntaxError is thrown #Not quite. It is valid syntax; it just returns nothing.
}

::o2 returning an object::What must you add to return an object literal from a concise arrow function? {
=Parentheses around the object literal #Correct. Otherwise the opening brace is read as a block body.
~The return keyword outside the braces #Not quite. return cannot appear in a concise body.
~A semicolon before the object #Not quite. A semicolon does not change how the brace is parsed.
~Nothing extra #Not quite. Without parentheses the braces are treated as a block.
}

::o3 timer with zero delay::Which statement about a callback passed to setTimeout with a delay of 0 is true? {
=It runs after the current code has finished #Correct. A timer callback always waits for the current code to complete.
~It runs before the next line #Not quite. That would make it synchronous, and it is not.
~It never runs #Not quite. It does run, just not immediately.
~It runs immediately and blocks the next line #Not quite. setTimeout returns first; the callback comes later.
}
